-- CISSP (ISC2 2024) starter questions across all 8 domains.
insert into public.questions (id, cert, domain, objective, qtype, difficulty, stem, choices, answer, explanation) values
-- Domain 1: Security & Risk Management
('ci-q-001','cissp',1,'1.2','mcq',2,$q$An organization researches threats and regulations before launching a product but implements no controls. Which concept did they satisfy, and which did they FAIL?$q$,$q$["Satisfied due care; failed due diligence","Satisfied due diligence; failed due care","Satisfied both","Failed both"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Due diligence is investigating and understanding risk (research/planning) - satisfied here. Due care is acting on it with reasonable protection - failed, since no controls were implemented. Diligence detects, care acts.$q$),
('ci-q-002','cissp',1,'1.3','mcq',3,$q$An asset worth $500,000 would lose 40% in a fire that historically occurs once every 25 years. What is the ALE?$q$,$q$["$200,000","$8,000","$20,000","$5,000"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$SLE = $500,000 x 0.40 = $200,000. ARO = 1/25 = 0.04. ALE = SLE x ARO = $200,000 x 0.04 = $8,000 per year - the justifiable annual spend to fully mitigate.$q$),
('ci-q-003','cissp',1,'1.3','mcq',2,$q$Who must formally accept residual risk after controls are applied?$q$,$q$["The security analyst","Senior management (the business)","The auditor","The vendor"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Residual risk is a business exposure; only senior management has the authority and accountability to accept it against the organization's risk appetite. Analysts implement controls; auditors assess; vendors don't own your risk.$q$),
('ci-q-004','cissp',1,'1.3','mcq',2,$q$A company buys cyber insurance to cover breach costs. Which risk treatment is this?$q$,$q$["Mitigation","Transfer","Avoidance","Acceptance"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Insurance shifts financial impact to a third party - transference. Mitigation adds controls, avoidance eliminates the activity, and acceptance means bearing the risk without action.$q$),
('ci-q-005','cissp',1,'1.1','mcq',3,$q$Under the (ISC)2 Code of Ethics, if protecting the public conflicts with a client's instruction, which takes precedence?$q$,$q$["The client's instruction","Protecting society and the public trust","Advancing the profession","Whichever is cheaper"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$The canons are ordered; "Protect society, the common good, and the public trust" comes first and takes precedence over duty to principals (clients). Order resolves conflicts in the code.$q$),
('ci-q-006','cissp',1,'1.4','mcq',2,$q$Which document is mandatory and states specific requirements like "AES-256 must be used"?$q$,$q$["Guideline","Standard","Procedure","Policy"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$A standard is mandatory and specifies concrete requirements. A policy states high-level intent, a procedure gives step-by-step how-to, and a guideline is recommended but not mandatory.$q$),
('ci-q-007','cissp',1,'1.5','mcq',2,$q$Under GDPR, an entity that determines the purpose and means of processing personal data is the:$q$,$q$["Data processor","Data controller","Data custodian","Data subject"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$The controller decides why and how personal data is processed and bears primary accountability. The processor acts on the controller's instructions; the custodian maintains it; the subject is the individual.$q$),
('ci-q-008','cissp',1,'1.3','mcq',2,$q$What does a Business Impact Analysis primarily produce?$q$,$q$["Firewall rules","Critical function identification with MTD, RTO, and RPO","Encryption keys","Marketing plans"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$The BIA identifies critical functions and the impact of disruption over time, producing recovery metrics (MTD, RTO, RPO) that drive BCP/DRP. It does not produce technical artifacts like keys or rules.$q$),
-- Domain 2: Asset Security
('ci-q-009','cissp',2,'2.1','mcq',2,$q$Who is accountable for assigning the classification of a data asset?$q$,$q$["Data custodian","Data owner","End user","Network engineer"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$The data owner is accountable and sets the classification and handling requirements. The custodian implements protection day-to-day; end users and engineers follow the owner's classification.$q$),
('ci-q-010','cissp',2,'2.4','mcq',2,$q$A hospital must release drives to an external recycler with the strongest assurance of data destruction. Which method?$q$,$q$["A single overwrite pass","Physical destruction (shredding/incineration)","Deleting files","Reformatting"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Physical destruction provides the highest assurance, appropriate for sensitive data leaving control. Overwriting/clearing suits reuse in the same environment; deletion and reformat leave recoverable remnants (data remanence).$q$),
('ci-q-011','cissp',2,'2.3','mcq',2,$q$Which data state is generally the HARDEST to protect?$q$,$q$["Data at rest","Data in transit","Data in use","Archived data"]$q$::jsonb,'2'::jsonb,$q$Data in use must be decrypted in memory for processing, exposing it to memory scraping and privileged malware. At rest and in transit are readily protected with encryption; in use relies on access control and enclaves.$q$),
('ci-q-012','cissp',2,'2.2','mcq',2,$q$Removing controls from a baseline that do not apply to your environment is called:$q$,$q$["Tailoring","Scoping","Hardening","Accreditation"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Scoping removes inapplicable controls. Tailoring adjusts or customizes the remaining baseline (adding, modifying, adding compensating controls) to fit risk and environment.$q$),
('ci-q-013','cissp',2,'2.4','mcq',3,$q$What is data remanence?$q$,$q$["Encryption metadata","Residual data remaining after deletion attempts","A backup schedule","A classification label"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Data remanence is residual data left on media after normal deletion, recoverable unless properly cleared, purged, or destroyed. It's why simple deletion is insufficient for sensitive data.$q$),
('ci-q-014','cissp',2,'2.2','mcq',2,$q$A regulation requires citizens' personal data to remain stored within the country. Which concept is this?$q$,$q$["Data remanence","Data sovereignty","Data masking","Tokenization"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Data sovereignty means data is subject to the laws of the country where it resides, driving in-country storage requirements. The other terms describe protection or leftover-data concepts.$q$),
-- Domain 3: Security Architecture & Engineering
('ci-q-015','cissp',3,'3.1','mcq',3,$q$Which model enforces confidentiality with "no read up, no write down"?$q$,$q$["Biba","Bell-LaPadula","Clark-Wilson","Brewer-Nash"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Bell-LaPadula protects confidentiality: subjects can't read above their clearance (no read up) or write to a lower level (no write down). Biba protects integrity; Clark-Wilson uses transactions; Brewer-Nash prevents conflicts of interest.$q$),
('ci-q-016','cissp',3,'3.1','mcq',3,$q$Which model protects integrity with "no read down, no write up"?$q$,$q$["Bell-LaPadula","Biba","Take-Grant","Graham-Denning"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Biba protects integrity: no reading lower-integrity data (no read down) and no writing to higher-integrity data (no write up). Bell-LaPadula is its confidentiality counterpart.$q$),
('ci-q-017','cissp',3,'3.2','mcq',2,$q$Which provides integrity, authenticity, and non-repudiation for a message?$q$,$q$["Symmetric encryption","A digital signature","Hashing alone","Base64 encoding"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$A digital signature (hash encrypted with the sender's private key) proves the message is unaltered (integrity), from the sender (authenticity), and undeniable (non-repudiation). Symmetric encryption gives confidentiality; hashing alone gives integrity but not authenticity/non-repudiation; encoding gives none.$q$),
('ci-q-018','cissp',3,'3.3','mcq',3,$q$A reference monitor must be tamperproof, always invoked, and small enough to verify. What implements it?$q$,$q$["The security kernel","The firewall","The hypervisor","The DMZ"]$q$::jsonb,'0'::jsonb,$q$The security kernel implements the reference monitor concept within the TCB, mediating all access with those three properties. Firewalls, hypervisors, and DMZs are architectural components, not the reference monitor.$q$),
('ci-q-019','cissp',3,'3.2','mcq',2,$q$What does perfect forward secrecy guarantee?$q$,$q$["Faster encryption","Compromise of a long-term key cannot decrypt past sessions","No need for certificates","Symmetric-only key exchange"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$PFS uses ephemeral session keys, so stealing a long-term private key later cannot decrypt previously recorded sessions. It doesn't speed encryption or remove certificates.$q$),
('ci-q-020','cissp',3,'3.2','mcq',2,$q$Which correctly describes symmetric vs asymmetric cryptography?$q$,$q$["Symmetric uses a key pair; asymmetric uses one key","Symmetric is fast with one shared key; asymmetric uses a public/private pair for exchange and signatures","They are identical","Asymmetric is only for bulk data"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Symmetric uses one shared key and is fast (bulk data); asymmetric uses a public/private key pair, slower, for key exchange and digital signatures. Hybrid systems use asymmetric to share a symmetric session key.$q$),
('ci-q-021','cissp',3,'3.4','mcq',2,$q$Which fire suppression approach is preferred for a data center to protect electronics?$q$,$q$["Wet pipe sprinklers directly on equipment","Clean agent (e.g., FM-200) or CO2 systems","Leaving fires to burn out","Class A water only"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Clean agents and CO2 suppress fire without the conductive damage water causes to electronics (Class C). Pre-action sprinklers reduce accidental discharge; plain water on energized equipment is hazardous.$q$),
-- Domain 4: Communication & Network Security
('ci-q-022','cissp',4,'4.1','ordering',2,$q$Order the OSI model layers from Layer 1 to Layer 7.$q$,$q$["Physical","Data Link","Network","Transport","Session","Presentation","Application"]$q$::jsonb,$q$[0,1,2,3,4,5,6]$q$::jsonb,$q$OSI layers 1-7: Physical, Data Link, Network, Transport, Session, Presentation, Application ("Please Do Not Throw Sausage Pizza Away"). Security controls and attacks map to specific layers.$q$),
('ci-q-023','cissp',4,'4.2','mcq',3,$q$Which IPsec component provides confidentiality (encryption) in addition to integrity?$q$,$q$["AH (Authentication Header)","ESP (Encapsulating Security Payload)","IKE only","GRE"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$ESP provides encryption plus integrity; AH provides integrity/authentication only, no encryption. IKE negotiates keys; GRE is an unencrypted tunneling protocol.$q$),
('ci-q-024','cissp',4,'4.2','mcq',2,$q$Which distinguishes a stateful firewall from a simple packet filter?$q$,$q$["It inspects Layer 7 content","It tracks connection state and allows valid return traffic","It only blocks ICMP","It requires no rules"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$A stateful firewall tracks connection state, automatically permitting legitimate return traffic and detecting out-of-state packets. A packet filter evaluates each packet against static rules without state.$q$),
('ci-q-025','cissp',4,'4.1','mcq',2,$q$What is the primary security benefit of network segmentation?$q$,$q$["Faster internet","Limiting lateral movement and containing compromise","Cheaper hardware","Eliminating the need for encryption"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Segmentation divides the network into zones so a compromise in one cannot easily spread - foundational to least privilege and zero trust. It doesn't replace encryption or inherently cut cost.$q$),
('ci-q-026','cissp',4,'4.3','mcq',2,$q$Which is the correct secure replacement for Telnet?$q$,$q$["FTP","SSH","HTTP","SNMPv1"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$SSH replaces Telnet with encrypted, authenticated remote access. FTP and HTTP are themselves cleartext protocols needing secure replacements; SNMPv1 is insecure.$q$),
('ci-q-027','cissp',4,'4.1','mcq',2,$q$Which principle defines zero-trust networking?$q$,$q$["Trust everything inside the perimeter","Never trust, always verify - authenticate/authorize every request by identity and context","Encrypt only external traffic","Rely solely on the firewall"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Zero trust assumes breach and verifies every request by identity and context regardless of network location, enforcing least privilege per session. Perimeter-only trust is the model it replaces.$q$),
-- Domain 5: Identity & Access Management
('ci-q-028','cissp',5,'5.1','ordering',2,$q$Order the IAAA access control steps.$q$,$q$["Identification","Authentication","Authorization","Accountability"]$q$::jsonb,$q$[0,1,2,3]$q$::jsonb,$q$IAAA: you claim an identity (identification), prove it (authentication), receive rights (authorization), and your actions are logged (accountability). Each step builds on the prior.$q$),
('ci-q-029','cissp',5,'5.2','mcq',2,$q$Which pair constitutes true multifactor authentication?$q$,$q$["Password and PIN","Password and a hardware token","Two security questions","A password typed twice"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Password (something you know) plus a hardware token (something you have) crosses two factor categories - true MFA. Passwords, PINs, and security questions are all "something you know," so combining them is single-factor.$q$),
('ci-q-030','cissp',5,'5.3','mcq',3,$q$A system enforces access using labels and clearances that users cannot override. Which model is this?$q$,$q$["Discretionary Access Control","Mandatory Access Control","Role-Based Access Control","Rule-Based Access Control"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$MAC is system-enforced via labels and clearances that owners/users cannot change - used in high-security/government contexts. DAC lets owners grant access; RBAC uses roles; rule-based applies conditions.$q$),
('ci-q-031','cissp',5,'5.2','mcq',3,$q$In biometrics, which metric represents impostors incorrectly accepted, and why is it the security concern?$q$,$q$["FRR - it locks out valid users","FAR - it admits unauthorized users","CER - it balances cost","Throughput - it measures speed"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$FAR (false acceptance rate) counts impostors wrongly admitted - a direct security failure. FRR is the usability failure (valid users denied); CER is where the two are equal, used to compare systems.$q$),
('ci-q-032','cissp',5,'5.4','mcq',2,$q$In SAML federated SSO, which party issues the signed authentication assertion?$q$,$q$["Service Provider","Identity Provider","Certificate Authority","RADIUS client"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$The Identity Provider authenticates the user and issues the signed assertion; the Service Provider trusts it and never sees the password. CAs issue certificates; RADIUS clients are network devices.$q$),
('ci-q-033','cissp',5,'5.4','mcq',2,$q$What is the relationship between OAuth 2.0 and OpenID Connect?$q$,$q$["They are unrelated","OpenID Connect is an authentication layer built on OAuth 2.0 authorization","OAuth is authentication; OIDC is encryption","OIDC replaces TLS"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$OAuth 2.0 handles delegated authorization; OpenID Connect adds an identity/authentication layer on top of it for login. Neither is an encryption/transport protocol.$q$),
('ci-q-034','cissp',5,'5.1','mcq',2,$q$An audit finds active accounts for employees who left months ago. Which IAM process failed?$q$,$q$["Provisioning","Deprovisioning (offboarding)","Authentication","Encryption"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Prompt deprovisioning on departure prevents orphaned accounts, which are unmonitored valid credentials for attackers or ex-insiders. The failure is in offboarding, not provisioning or authentication.$q$),
-- Domain 6: Security Assessment & Testing
('ci-q-035','cissp',6,'6.1','mcq',2,$q$What distinguishes a penetration test from a vulnerability assessment?$q$,$q$["A pen test only scans","A pen test actively exploits weaknesses to demonstrate real impact","A vulnerability assessment exploits flaws","They are identical"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$A vulnerability assessment identifies and prioritizes weaknesses; a penetration test actively exploits and chains them to prove real-world impact under agreed rules of engagement.$q$),
('ci-q-036','cissp',6,'6.1','mcq',2,$q$A tester is given no prior knowledge of the target, simulating an external attacker. Which testing type is this?$q$,$q$["White-box","Gray-box","Black-box","Clear-box"]$q$::jsonb,'2'::jsonb,$q$Black-box testing provides no internal knowledge - the most attacker-realistic but slower and less complete. White-box has full knowledge/source; gray-box has partial knowledge.$q$),
('ci-q-037','cissp',6,'6.2','mcq',3,$q$Which report attests to a service organization's security, availability, and confidentiality controls and is restricted in distribution?$q$,$q$["SOC 1","SOC 2","SOC 3","PCI ROC"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$SOC 2 covers the Trust Services Criteria (security, availability, processing integrity, confidentiality, privacy) and is restricted-use. SOC 1 is financial-reporting controls; SOC 3 is a public summary; PCI ROC is card-data compliance.$q$),
('ci-q-038','cissp',6,'6.2','mcq',2,$q$Why is an independent third-party audit valued over a self-assessment?$q$,$q$["It is always cheaper","Independence removes conflict of interest and increases assurance","Self-assessments are illegal","It requires no evidence"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Independent audits carry more weight with regulators and customers because the assessor has no stake in the outcome. Self-assessments are useful preparation but the weakest assurance.$q$),
('ci-q-039','cissp',6,'6.1','mcq',2,$q$Which testing approach uses scripted synthetic transactions to proactively verify availability and performance?$q$,$q$["Passive monitoring","Synthetic (active) monitoring","Fuzzing","Code review"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Synthetic monitoring runs scripted transactions to test systems proactively before users are affected. Passive/real-user monitoring observes actual traffic; fuzzing and code review target code flaws.$q$),
-- Domain 7: Security Operations
('ci-q-040','cissp',7,'7.4','ordering',3,$q$Order evidence collection by volatility, most volatile first.$q$,$q$["CPU cache and registers","RAM","Network connections","Disk storage","Backups and archives"]$q$::jsonb,$q$[0,1,2,3,4]$q$::jsonb,$q$Order of volatility: CPU cache/registers vanish first, then RAM, then live network state, then disk, then archival backups. Collect most volatile first to avoid losing evidence at power-off.$q$),
('ci-q-041','cissp',7,'7.2','mcq',2,$q$Which RAID level provides striping with dual parity, surviving two simultaneous disk failures?$q$,$q$["RAID 0","RAID 1","RAID 5","RAID 6"]$q$::jsonb,'3'::jsonb,$q$RAID 6 uses dual parity and tolerates two disk failures. RAID 5 tolerates one, RAID 1 mirrors, and RAID 0 stripes with no redundancy (any failure loses data).$q$),
('ci-q-042','cissp',7,'7.3','mcq',2,$q$What is the difference between BCP and DRP?$q$,$q$["They are identical","BCP keeps the whole business running; DRP is the IT restoration subset","DRP covers marketing; BCP covers IT","BCP is only for natural disasters"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$The Business Continuity Plan sustains overall business operations during disruption; the Disaster Recovery Plan is the IT-focused subset that restores systems and data. DRP supports BCP.$q$),
('ci-q-043','cissp',7,'7.3','mcq',2,$q$A function must resume within an hour with near-current data. Which recovery site is appropriate?$q$,$q$["Cold site","Warm site","Hot site","No site"]$q$::jsonb,'2'::jsonb,$q$A hot site is fully equipped with near-real-time data, meeting a tight RTO/RPO (minutes-hours) at higher cost. Warm sites have stale data (hours-days); cold sites take days-weeks.$q$),
('ci-q-044','cissp',7,'7.2','mcq',3,$q$Which pair of controls best prevents and detects fraud by a single privileged employee?$q$,$q$["Longer passwords and MFA","Separation of duties and job rotation","Encryption and hashing","Backups and RAID"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Separation of duties ensures no one person controls a full sensitive process (prevention); job rotation surfaces irregularities and reduces single-person dependence (detection). Crypto and backups address other risks.$q$),
('ci-q-045','cissp',7,'7.1','mcq',2,$q$What distinguishes an incident from a breach?$q$,$q$["Nothing","An incident threatens CIA; a breach is confirmed unauthorized access/disclosure of protected data","A breach is minor; an incident is major","A breach never requires notification"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$An incident is an event harming or threatening CIA; a breach is a confirmed unauthorized access to or disclosure of protected data, often triggering legal notification. Not every incident is a breach.$q$),
('ci-q-046','cissp',7,'7.3','mcq',2,$q$Backups run full on Sunday and differential daily. A failure occurs Thursday. Which backups restore the system?$q$,$q$["Sunday full plus every daily backup","Sunday full plus Wednesday's differential","Only Wednesday's differential","Only Sunday's full"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Differentials capture all changes since the last full, so restore needs the full plus only the most recent differential (Sunday + Wednesday). Incrementals would require the full plus every incremental.$q$),
-- Domain 8: Software Development Security
('ci-q-047','cissp',8,'8.1','mcq',2,$q$What is the primary goal of a Secure SDLC?$q$,$q$["Ship faster with no testing","Build security into every phase rather than bolting it on at the end","Eliminate documentation","Outsource all coding"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$A Secure SDLC embeds security in requirements, design, coding, testing, and maintenance - "shift left" - so flaws are caught early and cheaply. It doesn't remove testing or documentation.$q$),
('ci-q-048','cissp',8,'8.2','mcq',2,$q$Which testing analyzes source code without executing it?$q$,$q$["DAST","SAST","Penetration testing","Fuzzing"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$SAST (static analysis) examines source/binary without running it, early in development. DAST, pen testing, and fuzzing all exercise a running application.$q$),
('ci-q-049','cissp',8,'8.2','mcq',2,$q$What is the MOST effective defense against SQL injection?$q$,$q$["Client-side validation only","Parameterized queries (prepared statements) with input validation","Longer passwords","Disabling logging"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Parameterized queries separate code from data so input can't alter query logic, backed by server-side input validation and least-privilege DB accounts. Client-side checks are bypassable; passwords and logging are unrelated.$q$),
('ci-q-050','cissp',8,'8.1','mcq',2,$q$How does DevSecOps differ from traditional Waterfall for security?$q$,$q$["It removes security entirely","It automates and integrates security continuously into the CI/CD pipeline","It tests security only at the end","It is slower"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$DevSecOps automates security throughout continuous integration/delivery, catching issues on every change. Waterfall tests security late, making fixes costly and slow.$q$),
('ci-q-051','cissp',8,'8.3','mcq',2,$q$What is the purpose of software configuration management?$q$,$q$["Marketing the software","Tracking and controlling code/version/dependency changes for reproducible, auditable releases","Encrypting the database","Load balancing"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Configuration management controls changes to code, versions, and dependencies (baselines, versioning) so releases are reproducible, auditable, and free of unauthorized modification. It's not marketing, encryption, or balancing.$q$),
('ci-q-052','cissp',8,'8.3','mcq',3,$q$Which tool identifies known-vulnerable third-party open-source components in your application?$q$,$q$["SAST","Software Composition Analysis (SCA)","A network firewall","A SIEM"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$SCA inventories and flags vulnerable/improperly licensed third-party and open-source dependencies - the software supply-chain layer. SAST scans your own code; firewalls and SIEMs are runtime/monitoring tools.$q$),
('ci-q-053','cissp',8,'8.2','mcq',2,$q$Why is insecure deserialization dangerous?$q$,$q$["It slows the app","Untrusted serialized data can lead to remote code execution or object injection","It only affects logs","It improves performance"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$Deserializing attacker-controlled data without validation can execute arbitrary code or inject objects. Mitigate by validating/signing serialized data or avoiding native deserialization of untrusted input.$q$),
('ci-q-054','cissp',1,'1.3','mcq',2,$q$Which correctly orders the calculation for annual loss?$q$,$q$["ALE then SLE then ARO","Asset Value x Exposure Factor = SLE; SLE x ARO = ALE","ARO x Exposure Factor = ALE","SLE = ALE x ARO"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$SLE = Asset Value x Exposure Factor (loss per event); ALE = SLE x ARO (expected annual loss). ALE justifies how much to spend annually on a safeguard.$q$),
('ci-q-055','cissp',3,'3.1','mcq',2,$q$What is the Trusted Computing Base (TCB)?$q$,$q$["The list of trusted users","The totality of hardware, software, and firmware that enforces the security policy","The backup system","The DMZ"]$q$::jsonb,'1'::jsonb,$q$The TCB is all the components (hardware, software, firmware) responsible for enforcing security policy. Keeping it small and verifiable reduces the trusted attack surface. It is not a user list, backup, or network zone.$q$);
